{"iter": 2, "reply": "# FILE: spend/__init__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"spend \u2014 a small but real personal-expense-tracker CLI.\n\nBuilt feature-by-feature where early decisions constrain later ones. The\nload-bearing decision is the storage schema (see ``spend.models``): every\nentry has a stable integer ``id`` plus ``category`` and a ``tags`` *list*.\nThat list \u2014 rather than a packed string \u2014 is what makes tag filtering,\nCSV round-tripping, and the v1->v2 migration all behave.\n\nPublic surface (kept intentionally small and conventional):\n\n    from spend import Entry, Tracker, Config\n    from spend import load_config, save_config\n    from spend import csvexport, export_csv, read_csv\n    from spend import migrate\n    from spend import store, core\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    SpendError,\n    ValidationError,\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ConfigError,\n)\nfrom .models import (\n    Entry,\n    SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n    parse_date,\n    normalize_date,\n    today_iso,\n)\nfrom .config import (\n    Config,\n    load_config,\n    save_config,\n    default_config_path,\n    default_data_path,\n)\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\nfrom .csvexport import export_csv, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom . import models\nfrom . import config\nfrom . import migrations\nfrom . import tracker\nfrom . import store\nfrom . import csvexport\nfrom . import core\n\n# Backwards/alternate-name aliases so callers can import the CSV helpers under\n# either spelling without surprises.\ncsv_export = csvexport\n\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"Entry\",\n    \"Tracker\",\n    \"Config\",\n    \"SCHEMA_VERSION\",\n    \"DEFAULT_CATEGORY\",\n    \"parse_date\",\n    \"normalize_date\",\n    \"today_iso\",\n    \"load_config\",\n    \"save_config\",\n    \"default_config_path\",\n    \"default_data_path\",\n    \"migrate\",\n    \"export_csv\",\n    \"read_csv\",\n    \"TAG_SEPARATOR\",\n    \"csvexport\",\n    \"csv_export\",\n    \"store\",\n    \"core\",\n    \"errors\",\n    \"models\",\n    \"config\",\n    \"migrations\",\n    \"tracker\",\n    \"SpendError\",\n    \"ValidationError\",\n    \"EntryNotFoundError\",\n    \"NothingToUndoError\",\n    \"ConfigError\",\n    \"__version__\",\n]\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/errors.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Exception hierarchy for the spend package.\n\nA single base class (``SpendError``) lets callers and the CLI catch every\ndomain error in one place while still distinguishing specific failures.\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass SpendError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all errors raised by this package.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"An entry, config value, or argument failed validation.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass EntryNotFoundError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"No entry with the requested id exists.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass NothingToUndoError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"``undo`` was called but the undo stack is empty.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ConfigError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"The config file could not be read or contained bad values.\"\"\"\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/models.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 1 \u2014 the load-bearing decision: the data model / storage schema.\n\nAn :class:`Entry` is the atomic record. The fields chosen here ripple through\nevery later feature:\n\n* ``id``        \u2014 a stable integer identity. *Required* so that edit (turn 5),\n                  delete (turn 6) and undo (turn 12) can refer to a specific\n                  record across process restarts. Never reused.\n* ``amount``    \u2014 a float (money). Summary stats (turn 9) round to cents.\n* ``description`` \u2014 free text.\n* ``date``      \u2014 stored canonically as ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` regardless of the\n                  user's display ``date_format`` (turn 11). Canonical storage is\n                  what makes date-range queries (turn 8) and migration (turn 13)\n                  simple and unambiguous.\n* ``category``  \u2014 single string (turn 7). Defaults via config (turn 11).\n* ``tags``      \u2014 a *list* of strings (turn 7).\n\nWhy ``tags`` is a list and not a packed ``\"a,b\"`` string is the crux of turn 15:\na single packed string can't represent a tag that itself contains the packing\ndelimiter, and it forces every consumer (filter, CSV export, migration) to agree\non splitting rules. A real list defers the serialization choice to the boundary\n(the CSV writer) where it can be quoted/escaped correctly.\n\nThe schema is *additive over v1*: ``from_dict`` tolerates records written before\n``category``/``tags`` existed, supplying defaults. The on-disk wrapper version is\n``SCHEMA_VERSION``; the v1->v2 upgrade lives in ``spend.migrations``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport datetime\nimport math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\n\n# On-disk container format version. v1 was a bare JSON list of entry dicts with\n# no category/tags; v2 is a dict wrapper carrying next_id + entries.\nSCHEMA_VERSION = 2\n\nDEFAULT_CATEGORY = \"uncategorized\"\n\n\ndef today_iso() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return today's date as an ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()\n\n\ndef normalize_date(value: Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Coerce ``value`` to a canonical ISO date string, validating it.\n\n    Accepts a ``datetime.date``/``datetime.datetime`` or an ISO string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return today_iso()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.datetime):\n        return value.date().isoformat()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.date):\n        return value.isoformat()\n    s = str(value).strip()\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(s).isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"date must be ISO YYYY-MM-DD, got {value!r}\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\ndef parse_date(value: Any, fmt: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied date in ``fmt`` (or ISO) into a canonical ISO string.\n\n    Used by the CLI (turn 11): the user may type dates in their configured\n    ``date_format``, but storage stays ISO.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, (datetime.date, datetime.datetime)):\n        return normalize_date(value)\n    s = str(value).strip()\n    # ISO is always accepted regardless of the configured display format.\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(s).isoformat()\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n    try:\n        return datetime.datetime.strptime(s, fmt).date().isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"could not parse date {value!r} with format {fmt!r} or ISO\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Entry:\n    \"\"\"A single tracked expense.\"\"\"\n\n    id: int\n    amount: float\n    description: str = \"\"\n    date: str = field(default_factory=today_iso)\n    category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    tags: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        # Keep the in-memory shape sane without forcing every caller to call\n        # validate() explicitly.\n        if self.tags is None:\n            self.tags = []\n        elif not isinstance(self.tags, list):\n            self.tags = list(self.tags)\n\n    def validate(self) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Raise :class:`ValidationError` if the entry is malformed; returns self.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.id, int) or isinstance(self.id, bool):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"id must be an int, got {self.id!r}\")\n        try:\n            amount = float(self.amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"amount must be numeric, got {self.amount!r}\"\n            ) from exc\n        if not math.isfinite(amount):\n            raise ValidationError(\"amount must be finite\")\n        self.amount = amount\n        self.date = normalize_date(self.date)\n        if not isinstance(self.tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n        self.tags = [str(t) for t in self.tags]\n        self.category = str(self.category) if self.category else DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        self.description = \"\" if self.description is None else str(self.description)\n        return self\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Serialize to a JSON-safe dict with a stable field order.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"id\": self.id,\n            \"date\": self.date,\n            \"amount\": self.amount,\n            \"category\": self.category,\n            \"tags\": list(self.tags),\n            \"description\": self.description,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Build an Entry from a stored dict.\n\n        Tolerant of the turn-1 (v1) shape: ``category`` and ``tags`` may be\n        absent or null and are defaulted. This is what lets turn-7's schema\n        extension read turn-4's already-persisted data without breaking.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(data)!r}\"\n            )\n\n        raw_amount = data.get(\"amount\", 0.0)\n        try:\n            amount = float(raw_amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(\n                f\"amount must be numeric, got {raw_amount!r}\"\n            ) from exc\n\n        category = data.get(\"category\")\n        if not category:\n            category = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n        tags = data.get(\"tags\")\n        if tags is None:\n            tags = []\n        if not isinstance(tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n\n        date = data.get(\"date\")\n        if date is None:\n            date = today_iso()\n\n        ident = data.get(\"id\")\n        return cls(\n            id=int(ident) if ident is not None else 0,\n            amount=amount,\n            description=str(data.get(\"description\", \"\") or \"\"),\n            date=normalize_date(date),\n            category=str(category),\n            tags=[str(t) for t in tags],\n        )\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/config.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 11 \u2014 a config file (default category, date format).\n\nThe config is deliberately tiny and JSON-backed. Two settings matter to the\nrest of the app:\n\n* ``default_category`` \u2014 used by ``Tracker.add`` when the caller doesn't pass\n  one (so it must agree with turn-7's category model).\n* ``date_format`` \u2014 the *display/parse* format the CLI offers the user. Storage\n  stays ISO; this only affects parsing user input and formatting output.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import ConfigError\nfrom .models import DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef default_config_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Conventional location of the config file.\"\"\"\n    base = os.environ.get(\"SPEND_HOME\") or os.path.join(\n        os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\"\n    )\n    return os.path.join(base, \"config.json\")\n\n\ndef default_data_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Conventional location of the data file.\"\"\"\n    base = os.environ.get(\"SPEND_HOME\") or os.path.join(\n        os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\"\n    )\n    return os.path.join(base, \"data.json\")\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    \"\"\"User configuration.\"\"\"\n\n    default_category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    date_format: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"default_category\": self.default_category,\n            \"date_format\": self.date_format,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Optional[Dict[str, Any]]) -> \"Config\":\n        if data is None:\n            return cls()\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ConfigError(f\"config must be a JSON object, got {type(data)!r}\")\n        default_category = data.get(\"default_category\") or DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        date_format = data.get(\"date_format\") or \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n        return cls(\n            default_category=str(default_category),\n            date_format=str(date_format),\n        )\n\n\ndef load_config(path: Optional[str] = None) -> Config:\n    \"\"\"Load config from ``path`` (or the default). Missing file -> defaults.\"\"\"\n    if path is None:\n        path = default_config_path()\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return Config()\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            raw = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not read config at {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return Config.from_dict(raw)\n\n\ndef save_config(config: Config, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Persist ``config`` to ``path`` (or the default). Returns the path.\"\"\"\n    if path is None:\n        path = default_config_path()\n    if isinstance(config, dict):\n        config = Config.from_dict(config)\n    parent = os.path.dirname(path)\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        json.dump(config.to_dict(), fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)\n    return path\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/migrations.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 13 \u2014 migration that upgrades turn-1 (v1) data to the current schema.\n\nTwo historical on-disk shapes must be accepted:\n\n* **v1** (turns 1-6): the file was a *bare JSON list* of entry dicts, with no\n  ``category``/``tags`` and no top-level ``next_id``.\n* **v2** (turn 7 onward): a dict wrapper\n  ``{\"schema_version\", \"next_id\", \"entries\": [...]}``.\n\n``migrate`` is a pure function over already-parsed JSON. It is idempotent: a v2\ndocument passes through unchanged (aside from filling defaults). The important\ncorrectness point for turn 13 is reconstructing ``next_id`` for v1 data \u2014 v1\nnever stored it, so we derive ``max(id) + 1`` to guarantee future ids don't\ncollide with existing ones.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef _normalize_entry(record: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Bring a single stored entry dict up to the v2 field set (additively).\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(record, dict):\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(record)!r}\"\n        )\n    out = dict(record)\n    if not out.get(\"category\"):\n        out[\"category\"] = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    if out.get(\"tags\") is None:\n        out[\"tags\"] = []\n    if not isinstance(out[\"tags\"], (list, tuple)):\n        raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n    out[\"tags\"] = [str(t) for t in out[\"tags\"]]\n    if \"description\" not in out or out[\"description\"] is None:\n        out[\"description\"] = \"\"\n    if \"amount\" not in out or out[\"amount\"] is None:\n        out[\"amount\"] = 0.0\n    return out\n\n\ndef migrate(raw: Any) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Return a canonical v2 document from any supported historical shape.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(raw, list):\n        # v1: a bare list of entries.\n        entries: List[Any] = raw\n        next_id = None\n    elif isinstance(raw, dict):\n        entries = raw.get(\"entries\", [])\n        next_id = raw.get(\"next_id\")\n        if not isinstance(entries, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"'entries' must be a list\")\n    elif raw is None:\n        entries = []\n        next_id = None\n    else:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"unrecognized data file shape: {type(raw)!r}\")\n\n    normalized: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n    max_id = 0\n    for record in entries:\n        norm = _normalize_entry(record)\n        ident = norm.get(\"id\")\n        try:\n            ident_int = int(ident) if ident is not None else 0\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            ident_int = 0\n        norm[\"id\"] = ident_int\n        if ident_int > max_id:\n            max_id = ident_int\n        normalized.append(norm)\n\n    if next_id is None:\n        next_id = max_id + 1\n    else:\n        try:\n            next_id = int(next_id)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            next_id = max_id + 1\n        # Never hand out an id that would collide with existing data.\n        if next_id <= max_id:\n            next_id = max_id + 1\n\n    return {\n        \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n        \"next_id\": next_id,\n        \"entries\": normalized,\n    }\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/store.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 4 \u2014 persistence to disk (honoring the turn-1 schema).\n\nThe store is the boundary between JSON-on-disk and the in-memory object model.\nOn read it always runs the data through :func:`spend.migrations.migrate`, so a\nfile written under the v1 (turn-1) format is transparently upgraded before the\n:class:`~spend.tracker.Tracker` ever sees it.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION\n\n\ndef load(path: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Read ``path`` and return a canonical v2 document.\n\n    A missing file yields a fresh empty document so first-run callers don't\n    have to special-case it.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):\n        return {\"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION, \"next_id\": 1, \"entries\": []}\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            raw = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise SpendError(f\"could not read data file {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return migrate(raw)\n\n\ndef save(path: str, document: Dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Atomically write ``document`` (a v2 dict) to ``path``. Returns the path.\"\"\"\n    document = dict(document)\n    document.setdefault(\"schema_version\", SCHEMA_VERSION)\n    parent = os.path.dirname(path)\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    tmp = path + \".tmp\"\n    with open(tmp, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        json.dump(document, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=False)\n    os.replace(tmp, path)\n    return path\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/csvexport.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 10 \u2014 export to CSV (serializing the turn-7 tags correctly).\n\nThe tags list is the reason this module exists as its own boundary. We join\ntags with :data:`TAG_SEPARATOR` *inside* a single CSV field, and rely on the\nstdlib ``csv`` module to quote/escape the field as a whole. Reading the file\nback splits on the same separator, reconstructing the exact list \u2014 which is the\nproperty a packed-string-in-the-model could not guarantee.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport io\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Iterable, List, Optional\n\nfrom .models import Entry\n\nTAG_SEPARATOR = \"|\"\n\nFIELDNAMES = [\"id\", \"date\", \"amount\", \"category\", \"tags\", \"description\"]\n\n\ndef _coerce_entries(entries: Any) -> List[Entry]:\n    \"\"\"Accept a Tracker, a list of Entry, or a list of dicts.\"\"\"\n    if entries is None:\n        return []\n    # A Tracker (or anything exposing `.entries`).\n    inner = getattr(entries, \"entries\", None)\n    if inner is not None and not isinstance(entries, (list, tuple)):\n        entries = inner\n    out: List[Entry] = []\n    for item in entries:\n        if isinstance(item, Entry):\n            out.append(item)\n        elif isinstance(item, dict):\n            out.append(Entry.from_dict(item))\n        else:\n            raise TypeError(f\"cannot export object of type {type(item)!r}\")\n    return out\n\n\ndef _encode_tags(tags: Iterable[str]) -> str:\n    return TAG_SEPARATOR.join(str(t) for t in (tags or []))\n\n\ndef _decode_tags(value: Optional[str]) -> List[str]:\n    if value is None:\n        return []\n    value = str(value)\n    if value == \"\":\n        return []\n    return [part for part in value.split(TAG_SEPARATOR) if part != \"\"]\n\n\ndef _write(handle, entries: List[Entry]) -> None:\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(handle, fieldnames=FIELDNAMES)\n    writer.writeheader()\n    for entry in entries:\n        data = entry.to_dict()\n        writer.writerow(\n            {\n                \"id\": data[\"id\"],\n                \"date\": data[\"date\"],\n                \"amount\": data[\"amount\"],\n                \"category\": data[\"category\"],\n                \"tags\": _encode_tags(data.get(\"tags\")),\n                \"description\": data.get(\"description\", \"\"),\n            }\n        )\n\n\ndef export_csv(entries: Any, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Write ``entries`` to CSV.\n\n    With ``path`` given, the CSV is written there and the path is returned.\n    With ``path`` omitted, the CSV text is returned as a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = _coerce_entries(entries)\n    if path is None:\n        buf = io.StringIO()\n        _write(buf, rows)\n        return buf.getvalue()\n    parent = os.path.dirname(path)\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(path, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        _write(fh, rows)\n    return path\n\n\ndef read_csv(source: str) -> List[Entry]:\n    \"\"\"Read entries back from a CSV file path or CSV text.\n\n    The inverse of :func:`export_csv`, reconstructing the tags list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if source is None:\n        return []\n    if os.path.sep in str(source) or os.path.exists(str(source)):\n        with open(source, \"r\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            text = fh.read()\n    elif \"\\n\" in str(source) or \",\" in str(source):\n        text = str(source)\n    elif os.path.exists(str(source)):\n        with open(source, \"r\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            text = fh.read()\n    else:\n        # Treat as a path even if it doesn't exist (let open raise a clear error).\n        with open(source, \"r\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            text = fh.read()\n\n    reader = csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(text))\n    entries: List[Entry] = []\n    for row in reader:\n        entries.append(\n            Entry.from_dict(\n                {\n                    \"id\": row.get(\"id\"),\n                    \"date\": row.get(\"date\"),\n                    \"amount\": row.get(\"amount\"),\n                    \"category\": row.get(\"category\"),\n                    \"tags\": _decode_tags(row.get(\"tags\")),\n                    \"description\": row.get(\"description\", \"\"),\n                }\n            )\n        )\n    return entries\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tracker.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turns 2-12 \u2014 the Tracker: the live object that owns entries and mutations.\n\nIt ties the model (turn 1) to persistence (turn 4) and layers on every command:\nadd (2), list/filter (3), edit (5), delete-with-confirmation (6), tags (7),\ndate-range (8), summary (9), and undo (12). Undo is implemented as an in-memory\njournal of *inverse* operations, which is why it must understand the exact\nmutation model of edit/delete.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Callable, Dict, List, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ValidationError,\n)\nfrom .models import (\n    Entry,\n    SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n    normalize_date,\n    parse_date,\n    today_iso,\n)\nfrom .config import Config\nfrom . import store\n\n_EDITABLE = (\"amount\", \"description\", \"date\", \"category\", \"tags\")\n\n\nclass Tracker:\n    \"\"\"In-memory expense tracker with optional disk persistence.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        path: Optional[str] = None,\n        config: Optional[Config] = None,\n        autosave: bool = True,\n    ) -> None:\n        self.path = path\n        self.config = config or Config()\n        self.autosave = autosave\n        self.entries: List[Entry] = []\n        self._next_id = 1\n        self._undo: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n        if path and os.path.exists(path):\n            self.load()\n\n    # ----- persistence (turn 4) ------------------------------------------\n    def load(self, path: Optional[str] = None) -> \"Tracker\":\n        path = path or self.path\n        if not path:\n            raise ValidationError(\"no path to load from\")\n        self.path = path\n        doc = store.load(path)\n        self.entries = [Entry.from_dict(d) for d in doc.get(\"entries\", [])]\n        self._next_id = int(doc.get(\"next_id\", self._derive_next_id()))\n        if self._next_id <= self._max_id():\n            self._next_id = self._max_id() + 1\n        return self\n\n    def to_document(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n            \"next_id\": self._next_id,\n            \"entries\": [e.to_dict() for e in self.entries],\n        }\n\n    def save(self, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n        path = path or self.path\n        if not path:\n            raise ValidationError(\"no path to save to\")\n        self.path = path\n        return store.save(path, self.to_document())\n\n    def _autosave(self) -> None:\n        if self.autosave and self.path:\n            self.save()\n\n    # ----- id allocation --------------------------------------------------\n    def _max_id(self) -> int:\n        return max((e.id for e in self.entries), default=0)\n\n    def _derive_next_id(self) -> int:\n        return self._max_id() + 1\n\n    def _alloc_id(self) -> int:\n        ident = self._next_id\n        self._next_id += 1\n        return ident\n\n    # ----- lookup ---------------------------------------------------------\n    def _index_of(self, entry_id: int) -> int:\n        for i, e in enumerate(self.entries):\n            if e.id == entry_id:\n                return i\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id!r}\")\n\n    def get(self, entry_id: int) -> Entry:\n        return self.entries[self._index_of(int(entry_id))]\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.entries)\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return iter(self.entries)\n\n    # ----- add (turn 2) ---------------------------------------------------\n    def add(\n        self,\n        amount: Any,\n        description: str = \"\",\n        date: Any = None,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    ) -> Entry:\n        entry = Entry(\n            id=self._alloc_id(),\n            amount=amount,\n            description=description or \"\",\n            date=normalize_date(date) if date is not None else today_iso(),\n            category=category if category else self.config.default_category,\n            tags=list(tags) if tags else [],\n        ).validate()\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        self._undo.append({\"op\": \"add\", \"id\": entry.id})\n        self._autosave()\n        return entry\n\n    # ----- list / filter (turns 3, 7, 8) ---------------------------------\n    def list(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n        start: Any = None,\n        end: Any = None,\n        description_contains: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> List[Entry]:\n        results = list(self.entries)\n        if category is not None:\n            results = [e for e in results if e.category == category]\n        if tag is not None:\n            results = [e for e in results if tag in e.tags]\n        if tags:\n            wanted = set(tags)\n            results = [e for e in results if wanted.issubset(set(e.tags))]\n        if start is not None:\n            start_iso = normalize_date(start)\n            results = [e for e in results if e.date >= start_iso]\n        if end is not None:\n            end_iso = normalize_date(end)\n            results = [e for e in results if e.date <= end_iso]\n        if description_contains:\n            needle = description_contains.lower()\n            results = [e for e in results if needle in e.description.lower()]\n        return results\n\n    def filter(self, **kwargs: Any) -> List[Entry]:\n        return self.list(**kwargs)\n\n    def all(self) -> List[Entry]:\n        return list(self.entries)\n\n    # ----- date-range query (turn 8) -------------------------------------\n    def between(self, start: Any, end: Any) -> List[Entry]:\n        return self.list(start=start, end=end)\n\n    def range(self, start: Any, end: Any) -> List[Entry]:\n        return self.between(start, end)\n\n    # ----- edit (turn 5) --------------------------------------------------\n    def edit(self, entry_id: int, **changes: Any) -> Entry:\n        idx = self._index_of(int(entry_id))\n        current = self.entries[idx]\n        before = current.to_dict()\n\n        updated = current.to_dict()\n        for key, value in changes.items():\n            if key not in _EDITABLE:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"cannot edit field {key!r}\")\n            if key == \"tags\":\n                updated[\"tags\"] = list(value) if value else []\n            elif key == \"date\":\n                updated[\"date\"] = normalize_date(value)\n            else:\n                updated[key] = value\n        new_entry = Entry.from_dict(updated).validate()\n        new_entry.id = current.id\n        self.entries[idx] = new_entry\n\n        self._undo.append({\"op\": \"edit\", \"id\": current.id, \"before\": before})\n        self._autosave()\n        return new_entry\n\n    # ----- delete with confirmation (turn 6) -----------------------------\n    def delete(\n        self,\n        entry_id: int,\n        confirm: Any = True,\n    ) -> Entry:\n        idx = self._index_of(int(entry_id))\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n\n        if callable(confirm):\n            if not confirm(entry):\n                raise ValidationError(\"deletion not confirmed\")\n        elif not confirm:\n            raise ValidationError(\"deletion not confirmed\")\n\n        removed = self.entries.pop(idx)\n        self._undo.append(\n            {\"op\": \"delete\", \"index\": idx, \"entry\": removed.to_dict()}\n        )\n        self._autosave()\n        return removed\n\n    # ----- summary stats (turn 9) ----------------------------------------\n    def summary(\n        self,\n        start: Any = None,\n        end: Any = None,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        rows = self.list(start=start, end=end, category=category)\n        total = round(sum(e.amount for e in rows), 2)\n        count = len(rows)\n        by_category: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        by_tag: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for e in rows:\n            by_category[e.category] = round(\n                by_category.get(e.category, 0.0) + e.amount, 2\n            )\n            for t in e.tags:\n                by_tag[t] = round(by_tag.get(t, 0.0) + e.amount, 2)\n        average = round(total / count, 2) if count else 0.0\n        amounts = [e.amount for e in rows]\n        return {\n            \"count\": count,\n            \"total\": total,\n            \"average\": average,\n            \"min\": round(min(amounts), 2) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"max\": round(max(amounts), 2) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"by_category\": by_category,\n            \"by_tag\": by_tag,\n        }\n\n    def total(self, **kwargs: Any) -> float:\n        return self.summary(**kwargs)[\"total\"]\n\n    # ----- undo (turn 12) -------------------------------------------------\n    def can_undo(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self._undo)\n\n    def undo(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        if not self._undo:\n            raise NothingToUndoError(\"nothing to undo\")\n        record = self._undo.pop()\n        op = record[\"op\"]\n        if op == \"add\":\n            # Inverse of add: remove the added entry (if still present).\n            try:\n                idx = self._index_of(record[\"id\"])\n                self.entries.pop(idx)\n            except EntryNotFoundError:\n                pass\n        elif op == \"delete\":\n            # Inverse of delete: re-insert at its old position.\n            entry = Entry.from_dict(record[\"entry\"])\n            index = min(record.get(\"index\", len(self.entries)), len(self.entries))\n            self.entries.insert(index, entry)\n        elif op == \"edit\":\n            # Inverse of edit: restore the prior snapshot.\n            before = Entry.from_dict(record[\"before\"])\n            try:\n                idx = self._index_of(before.id)\n                self.entries[idx] = before\n            except EntryNotFoundError:\n                self.entries.append(before)\n        else:  # pragma: no cover - defensive\n            raise ValidationError(f\"unknown undo op {op!r}\")\n        self._autosave()\n        return record\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/core.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Helpers that glue the pieces together for programmatic and CLI use.\n\nThis is the thin orchestration layer: open a tracker against a data file using\na config, and a couple of convenience functions the CLI (and tests) can call\nwithout poking at internals.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .config import Config, load_config, default_data_path\nfrom .csvexport import export_csv\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\n\n\ndef open_tracker(\n    data_path: Optional[str] = None,\n    config_path: Optional[str] = None,\n    config: Optional[Config] = None,\n) -> Tracker:\n    \"\"\"Open (and migrate, if needed) the tracker at ``data_path``.\"\"\"\n    if config is None:\n        config = load_config(config_path)\n    path = data_path or default_data_path()\n    return Tracker(path=path, config=config)\n\n\ndef export(tracker: Tracker, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Export a tracker's entries to CSV.\"\"\"\n    return export_csv(tracker, path)\n\n\ndef summarize(tracker: Tracker, **kwargs) -> dict:\n    return tracker.summary(**kwargs)\n\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Minimal CLI entrypoint.\n\n    Kept intentionally small; the real behavior lives in :class:`Tracker`.\n    Supports: add, list, summary, export.\n    \"\"\"\n    import argparse\n\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"spend\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--data\", default=None)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--config\", default=None)\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\")\n\n    p_add = sub.add_parser(\"add\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"amount\", type=float)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"description\", nargs=\"?\", default=\"\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", dest=\"tags\", default=None)\n\n    sub.add_parser(\"list\")\n    sub.add_parser(\"summary\")\n\n    p_exp = sub.add_parser(\"export\")\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"path\")\n\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    tracker = open_tracker(args.data, args.config)\n\n    if args.command == \"add\":\n        entry = tracker.add(\n            args.amount,\n            description=args.description,\n            date=args.date,\n            category=args.category,\n            tags=args.tags,\n        )\n        print(f\"added #{entry.id}: {entry.amount} {entry.category}\")\n    elif args.command == \"list\":\n        for e in tracker.list():\n            print(f\"#{e.id} {e.date} {e.amount} {e.category} {','.join(e.tags)}\")\n    elif args.command == \"summary\":\n        s = tracker.summary()\n        print(f\"count={s['count']} total={s['total']} average={s['average']}\")\n    elif args.command == \"export\":\n        out = export(tracker, args.path)\n        print(f\"exported to {out}\")\n    else:\n        parser.print_help()\n        return 1\n    return 0\n```", "summary": "1 error"}